<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=ballpoint-pen">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ballpoint pen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1888">1888</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as biro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The ballpoint pen dispenses ink, usually in paste form, over a hard ball rolling in its point. The first patent was issued on 30 October 1888 to the American John J. Loud for a pen with a rotating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> ball, intended for marking boxes and packaging; it proved too coarse for letters and the patent lapsed. László Bíró, a Hungarian newspaper editor, coupled viscous ink like that of newspaper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> with a ball-and-socket mechanism, working with his brother György, and filed a British patent on 15 June 1938. The Reynolds Rocket, sold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> from 29 October 1945, was the first commercially successful ballpoint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ballpoint-pen.jpg" alt="Ballpoint pen" width="330" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trounce · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:03-BICcristal2008-03-26.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
